    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The buildup of lactic acid is just one part of the equation, and it's not the direct cause of muscle damage or soreness. The damage and soreness that follow intense exercise, especially HIIT, is often referred to as delayed onset muscle soreness (DOMS).\n\nDOMS is thought to be caused by micro-tears in the muscle fibers, which are tiny tears in the muscle tissue. When you engage in high-intensity exercise, the muscle fibers are subjected to intense stress, which can cause them to tear. This is especially true for the type II muscle fibers, which are responsible for generating force and power.\n\nThe micro-tears are thought to be caused by the rapid contraction and relaxation of the muscle fibers, which can cause them to stretch and tear. This process is often referred to as \"eccentric loading,\" where the muscle fibers are subjected to a prolonged period of lengthening under tension.\n\nThe lactic acid buildup, on the other hand, is more of a byproduct of the intense exercise itself. The lactic acid is produced as a result of the anaerobic respiration, and it's not directly responsible for the muscle damage.\n\nHowever, the lactic acid can contribute to the muscle damage in a few ways",
